{
  "image_name": "0b0667ab5c0c088b046027d0865699dd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how mitochondria maintain quality and communicate with the cell through their structure, dynamic remodeling, and vesicle-based disposal pathways. At the top, a schematic of mitochondrial membranes highlights the inner membrane cristae and organizing complexes such as MICOS/SAM, alongside the respiratory chain and ATP synthase, emphasizing mitochondria’s central roles in energy production, metabolism, calcium buffering, redox balance, apoptosis, and other cellular functions.\n\nThe middle portion focuses on mitochondrial dynamics. A balance between fusion and fission controls whether mitochondria remain elongated or become fragmented. Fusion is associated with content exchange, preservation of membrane potential, and adaptation to mild stress, whereas fission supports organelle degradation, mitosis, apoptosis, and response to hypoxia or severe stress. Damage can lead either to apoptosis, via Bax–Bak pores and cytochrome c release, or to mitophagy, where depolarized mitochondria are engulfed by a phagophore, enclosed in an autophagosome, and degraded after lysosome fusion.\n\nThe lower part shows more selective quality-control routes involving mitochondria-derived vesicles and mitochondrial-derived compartments. These carry mitochondrial proteins, antigens, or matrix components to multivesicular bodies, lysosomes, peroxisomes, or autophagosomes, linking mitochondrial stress to trafficking, immune presentation, and organelle turnover.",
